class_name DuelLore
## Every Totem's story: why its Shard took the form it did (4 November 2026,
## Gary: in the menu's Deck "I want it to give each totem a proper back story.
## Why that shard took that form etc"). Shown under the card in the Deck and
## the Codex (Codex.Detail), not in the duel's quick card view.
##
## The rule of the world: magic leaks out of the gods' war and settles in the
## mortal world as Shards; a Shard takes the shape of whatever it finds in the
## moment it lands, a living thing, a death, a want, a place, and that shape
## is the Totem. An Ascended card is the same Shard grown into more of what
## it was.

const BACKSTORY := {
	# --- fire -----------------------------------------------------------------
	"cinderpup": "The first Cinderpup was a farm dog that lay down in the warm ash of a burnt-out hayrick on the night of a Sigilfall over Ashford, and would not come away from it. The Shard that fell into the ash found a creature already half in love with fire and kept nothing else. Every Cinderpup since has that dog's habit of sleeping too close to the hearth, and its stubbornness: a pup that has decided a thing is worth guarding will burn before it moves.",
	"blazehound": "A Cinderpup left in a duellist's keeping for years, fed on the heat of every duel it fought, grows past the hearth it was made at. The Shard does not change its mind, only its size: the pup's loyalty becomes a hound's, the ember in its chest becomes a furnace, and it learns to take the fire out of what it kills and keep it. Shepherds on the Emberforge road call them 'the ones that come back warmer'.",
	"emberwisp": "Not a creature at all, once. Ysolde's fire is the kind that shows what is hidden, and a Shard of it fell into a lamp left burning in an empty room in Solhaven, over a letter nobody had the courage to open. The wisp is what the lamp became: a small flame that drifts to whatever is being kept from the light. Duellists keep them for the company and for the way they find things. Nobody has made one on purpose.",
	"hearth_salamander": "In the mountain villages above Emberforge every house keeps a salamander in the hearth, and a hearth that goes cold is a house that has given up. A Shard fell down a chimney in the hardest winter anyone there remembers and the salamander in that grate did not die with the family's last log; it ate the Shard and kept the fire going until spring. Its line still does the work of a hearth: it holds heat for others and gives it back when the fire is nearly out.",
	"ashen_drake": "When Pyrrhane's war first touched the mortal world, a whole hillside above Emberforge burned for nine days, and a Shard fell into the crater while the rock was still molten. What rose out of it was not a dragon in the old stories' sense but the fire's memory of the hill: scaled in slag, breathing the smoke of a forest that no longer exists. The Ashen Drake has never been seen to sleep. The smiths say it is still waiting for the hill to grow back so it can burn it again.",
	"infernal_warg": "Only a Blazehound that has outlived the duellist it was made for goes this far. Grief does something to a fire Shard: it stops warming and starts hungering. The Warg is a hound with nothing left to guard, so it guards the fire itself, and takes the heat out of everything it bites to feed a furnace that was once a pup asleep in the ash. Those who meet one in the Shardlands say its eyes are the only part of it still looking for someone.",
	# --- storm ------------------------------------------------------------------
	"sparkkit": "Ixara's storms break first over the moors north of Veilwild, where the foxes den in the peat. One lightning strike went down a fox hole instead of a tree and the vixen's youngest came out alive with its fur standing on end and crackling. The Shard in that strike had found the quickest thing on the moor and copied it. Sparkkits are never still; a kit that has been in a duel for three turns is a different animal from the one that was called, and faster.",
	"voltlynx": "A Sparkkit that is played often enough stops being small. The Shard follows the fox's own ambition, which was never to be a fox: it wanted to be the biggest hunter on the moor, and the lightning obliges. The Voltlynx keeps the kit's trick of moving before anything can answer it, but it has the weight now to make the first blow count. Moor folk say you hear the lynx before you see it, and by then it has already gone past.",
	"tempest_lynx": "The Storm Herald's own hunting cat, by the time it has grown this far. A Voltlynx that has lived through enough storms stops needing them: it carries the weather in its coat. The Tempest Lynx is what the moor's small fox always meant to become, and the Shard has given it exactly that, a thing nothing can outrun. They are called only by duellists who can keep up with them.",
	"thunder_ram": "On the high pastures of Ironstone the rams fight at the first thunder of spring, and the flocks' owners stand well back. A Shard came down in the middle of one of those fights and chose the ram that was winning. The Thunder Ram is that animal's bad temper given a storm to work with: it lowers its head at anything, it does not wait to be told, and the crack when it lands is heard in the next valley. Ironstone shepherds consider it a point of pride to have one.",
	"stormcaller": "The heron fishes the river at Ashford's mill in the hour before a storm, when the fish rise. The eldest of them was standing in the millrace when Ixara's first Sigilfall lit the valley, and it did not fly. The Shard that landed in the water at its feet took the heron's patience along with its spear of a beak, and something of the storm's own voice: a Stormcaller does not strike until the moment is right, and when it calls, the weather comes to it. Tam's grandmother fed the first one bread.",
	# --- tide ---------------------------------------------------------------------
	"brookfin": "The brooks that feed the Ashford river are full of little fin-dragons, so the children's stories went long before anyone had seen one. A Shard of Maerith's fell into the mill pool and found a brown trout that had been living under the wheel for years, too wily to be caught. What it made is the storybook thing: a fish with fins like a dragon's and a dragon's memory for its enemies. A Brookfin remembers who last hurt it.",
	"riptide_serpent": "A Brookfin that has fought enough duels learns what the sea knows: that the water does not need to be strong, only to be everywhere at once. The Shard lengthens the fish into a serpent and gives it the pull of a tide going out. Fishermen at Tidegrove know the sign of one, a flat calm with a current under it, and do not put the boats out.",
	"deepmaw": "There is a trench off Tidegrove where the fishing boats will not cast, and the Tide Mother's oldest Shard lies at the bottom of it in the shape of something that was a serpent once. The Deepmaw Leviathan is what the sea makes of a Riptide Serpent that has had too long in the dark and too much to eat: patient, enormous, and only ever called by duellists who have already decided the duel is worth drowning the field for.",
	"shellguard": "Tidegrove's harbour wall is built on turtle shells, by the old custom, and the harbour has never been breached. A Shard fell into the wall and found a turtle that had been part of it for two hundred years, and it did not make a monster: it made a wall that could move. The Shellguard puts itself between the sea and whatever is behind it, and the Totems beside it fight as if they had a harbour at their backs.",
	"river_hound": "The hounds that work the ferries at Ashford are bred to go in after a fallen child and bring it out, and they do not stop for the current. The ferryman's bitch went in after a Shard that fell in the river at the spring flood, because it was glowing and she thought it was a lantern, and came out wearing a ruff of white water that never dried. Tam's own River Hound is one of her line, and it will go in after anything you point it at.",
	# --- frost ---------------------------------------------------------------------
	"rimecub": "Vaelith's Shards fall where it is coldest, and the cold does not make things so much as keep them. A bear cub lost in the first snow of a Shardlands winter lay down to die under a glass spire and a Shard kept it: not alive, exactly, and not dead, but held at the moment before the end, with its mother's warmth still in its fur and the snow's cold in its breath. The Rimecub is gentle and will not grow. It bites to make things stop.",
	# --- verdant -----------------------------------------------------------------
	"mossling": "Verdanthe's magic lands softly. A Shard of hers settled on a stone in the Thornwood where the moss had grown for a thousand years, and the moss simply got up. A Mossling is slow and difficult to kill, because there is nothing in it to kill: it is the forest floor, deciding for once to go somewhere. Children in the Thornwood villages leave them on the windowsill in winter, and they keep the frost off the glass.",
	"thornwarden": "A Mossling that has been hurt often enough stops being soft. The Rootmother's answer to a wound is a thorn, and a Shard that has been in enough duels grows them: the Thornwarden is the forest's floor with the forest's defences, and anything that strikes it leaves some of itself behind on the briars. Thornwood woodsmen say it is the only Totem that gets kinder as it gets older, and only to its own side.",
	"briar_colossus": "When the briar has grown over the whole of what it was guarding, it is finished, and it stands up. The Briar Colossus is a Thornwarden that has nothing left to protect but itself, and so protects everything: a walking thicket the height of a chapel, armed on every side. The old road through the deep Thornwood is closed because one of them has decided to stand on it, and nobody has thought of a reason good enough to ask it to move.",
	"bloomsprite": "In the orchards above Solhaven the blossom comes out on one morning each year, all of it at once, and the whole town goes up to see. A Shard came down into the blossom on that morning and what it found was joy, which is rarer than it sounds. The Bloomsprite is a winged thing made of petals and the mood of that one day; it will not fight hard, but it tends what is beside it, and it is impossible to stay angry with.",
	"elderbark": "The oldest tree in the Thornwood had already died when the Shard struck it, and the Shard did not bring it back; it woke what was left. Elderbark is the memory of a forest, held in dead wood, slow to anger and nearly impossible to move. It guards because guarding is all a tree was ever doing. The Thornwood keepers will not cut within sight of one, and the one time somebody did, the saw is still there.",
	# --- earth ----------------------------------------------------------------------
	"pebblit": "Orrun's Shards are the commonest in the world, because stone is, and most of them come to nothing. One fell into a dry-stone wall above Ashford's lane and found a stone that a boy had been talking to for a whole summer, on the way to and from the mill. The Pebblit is a stone that has been loved enough to answer: small, round, patient, and far harder to break than anyone expects. It echoes, because that is what the boy taught it to do.",
	"boulderox": "A Pebblit that has been in enough duels gathers weight, as a rolling stone does not but a loved one will. The Stone Warden's magic builds slowly. The Boulderox is the little wall-stone grown to the size of the beast that pulls the quarry carts, with the same unhurried temper, and nothing in the mortal world pushes it back once it has set its feet. Quarrymen at Ironstone say the only way through one is to wait a thousand years.",
	"cragmaul": "The mountain's own hand, by the time a Boulderox has gone this far. Orrun does not make warriors, but a stone that has fought long enough learns what a landslide knows, which is that enough weight needs no cleverness. The Cragmaul is the Boulderox after the mountain has claimed it: a cliff that walks, and that remembers, somewhere under the rock, a boy on the lane who used to say good morning to it.",
	"burrowmole": "The Ironstone miners' saying is that the mountain is full of moles, and they mean the ones that find the ore. One such mole dug into a Shard where no ore had any business being and came out through the floor of the counting house a week later, three valleys away. A Burrowmole is never where it was: it comes up under things. Duellists who like surprises keep them; the ones who like plans do not.",
	"quarry_golem": "The quarry at Ironstone has a face that the masons will not cut, because it looks back. A Shard struck it in the first year of the war and the stone that had been watching the quarrymen for generations stood up out of the wall to see them better. The Quarry Golem guards the way a cliff guards the valley below it, without malice and without any intention of moving. Orrun's priests say it is the Stone Warden's own likeness. The masons say it has his manners.",
	# --- metal -----------------------------------------------------------------------
	"brassback": "Hethrin's Shards fall on the forges, and the forges are ready for them. A beetle crawled into the brass shavings under an apprentice's bench at Ironstone and a Shard crawled in after it; what the Forgefather's magic made of the two was the apprentice's first good piece, armoured in his own cast-offs. A Brassback is a small shield that walks and clanks, and it has never once got out of the way.",
	"ironhide": "The Brassback that is kept, oiled and fought grows into the armour the Forgefather meant it for. The Ironhide Warbeetle is a shield the size of a cart, and it still does the apprentice's one job: it stands in front. Ironstone's gate-wardens drill beside them. There is no Totem in the world easier to find in a duel, and none that minds less being found.",
	"ironclad_behemoth": "What the beetle becomes when the forge has finished with it. The Ironclad Behemoth is Hethrin's answer to the question of how much armour a living thing can carry, and the answer is all of it. There is still a beetle in there, by the Forgefather's own account; nobody has seen it in a generation. A Behemoth does not attack so much as arrive, and the field is different afterwards.",
	"shieldhound": "The mastiffs that guard Hethrin's vaults are bred for one thing, to stand, and a Shard fell into the vault one night while the oldest of them lay across the door. It had been standing there for eleven years. The Shieldhound is that dog's whole life made into a Totem: loyal past the point of sense, and a rallying point for anything that fights beside it. It will not die while there is something behind it to die for.",
	"gilded_sentinel": "In the Sun Court of Solhaven there is a statue of a knight in gold that the Forgefather made for Solmaris, when the gods still gave each other gifts. A Shard fell on it in the war's first year, perhaps by accident, perhaps not. The Gilded Sentinel is the gift remembering the giving: a guardian that shelters whatever stands beside it, and whose shadow falls the same whether the sun is up or down. It is Hethrin's, and it stands in Solmaris's house. Nobody at the Court will say what that means.",
	# --- psychic -------------------------------------------------------------------------
	"dreamfox": "Oriel's Shards fall into sleep. A fox that had curled up in the warm ash behind the Ashford bakehouse was dreaming of the hen-run when a Shard found it, and woke knowing the hens' dreams as well as its own. The Dreamfox is a thief, but it steals nothing that can be held: it takes the thing you were about to do and shows it to everyone. Duellists who play it learn to hide their plans even from themselves.",
	"veilfox": "A Dreamfox that has been shown enough minds learns to hide its own. The Veilfox is the fox with its dream turned inside out, so that what you see of it is what it wants you to see, and the blow you aimed at it lands on the place it was a moment ago. Veilwild is named for them, or they for it. Either way, the people there lock their doors against things that cannot be locked out.",
	"ninefold_fox": "The oldest Veilfox in Veilwild has nine shadows and nobody knows which one bites. Oriel's magic, given long enough, stops hiding the fox and starts hiding the world: a Ninefold Fox fights in a duel the way a dream fights sleep, from everywhere at once, and the answer to its question is always wrong. It is said that it remembers being a fox behind a bakehouse, and that this is the one thing it will not let anyone see.",
	"somnowl": "The owl that roosted in the Dreaming Eye's shrine at Veilwild had heard three hundred years of people asking the god for sleep. When the Shard fell through the shrine's broken roof it found a bird that had learned to give what was asked. A Somnowl's wings make no sound and its gaze makes no sense; those it looks at are asleep before they know they are tired, and they wake with the feeling that the duel went on without them.",
	"thought_eater": "Something that was never an animal. In the first year of the war a Shard of Oriel's came down on the scholars' hall at Veilwild while the whole college was at its desks, and what rose from the ink was the hunger that had been in that room: the need to know, with nothing left to want it. The Thought Eater takes what its victims were thinking and is a little larger afterwards. The scholars have not gone back to the hall.",
	# --- venom ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
	"blightrat": "Vexa's Shards fall where people have already given up, and the rats are there first. One came down into the sump of a plague-house at the edge of Solhaven, in the year the Court sealed the gates, and the rat it found had eaten everything else and lived. The Blightrat is the Plague Saint's cheapest gift: a thing that cannot be poisoned, because it is poison, and that echoes back whatever is done to it. Nobody breeds them. They do not need it.",
	"plague_asp": "A Blightrat that survives enough duels sheds its fur and keeps its venom, and what is left is the asp. Vexa's magic does not make its creatures stronger so much as more certain: the Plague Asp strikes once and the matter is decided, somewhere in the blood, over the next few turns. Physicians in Solhaven keep a drawing of one on the wall so that they will know what they are looking at.",
	"blight_wyrm": "A Plague Asp that has been fed enough is no longer a snake but a sickness with a shape. The Blight Wyrm is what the Plague Saint sends once patience is over, and the coils of it are the long slow way an illness takes a town. It will not win a duel quickly. It will not need to. There was a village on the Veilwild road where one was called and not called back, and the road goes round it now.",
	"mirewidow": "The bog between Thornwood and Veilwild takes a few travellers every year, and the spiders there have grown used to being fed. A Shard sank into the peat and found a widow that had been waiting at the centre of her web for longer than spiders live. The Mirewidow does not chase; it waits, and what it waits for is the moment you step somewhere you should not have. Duellists who use it well are patient people. The rest find out what the web is for.",
	# --- radiant -------------------------------------------------------------------------
	"dawnling": "Solmaris's Shards fall at first light, and the Sun Court's gardeners keep the lawns clear for them. A Dawnling is a scrap of the dawn with wings, the Radiant Sovereign's smallest gift, and the Court gives one to every child who takes the morning service. It rallies whatever it flies beside, because the first light always does. There is no malice in it, and no great strength; it is there to remind the field which way the sun comes up.",
	"sunmane_lion": "The lions carved on the Sunspire's gate were only carvings until the first Sigilfall of the war, when a Shard struck the gate at noon and one of them shook out its mane. The Sunmane Lion is the Court's pride and its warning: swift, golden, and very sure of whose ground it is on. It fights for Solmaris and sleeps on the Sunspire's steps. The gate has one lion carved on it now, and the masons have been told to leave it so.",
	"sunlance_seraph": "The Court's priests say the Seraph was never a Shard at all but a piece of the Radiant Sovereign herself, loosed when the throne first shook. Whatever it was, it came down through the Sunspire's roof as a spear of light and stood up out of the floor with wings. The Sunlance Seraph is called by the Court's champions alone, and only when the Sovereign's name is at stake. Few duellists have seen one. Fewer have seen one and gone on to win.",
	# --- spirit ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
	"lantern_moth": "On the night of Ashford's Lantern Festival the moths come down to the green by the hundred, to the paper lanterns the children carry, and one of the first Shards ever to fall on the village came down among them. Ellowen's magic lands on what is going home. The Lantern Moth is the festival's own creature: it tends the light beside it, it channels what it is given, and it will fly a long way in the dark for someone who has lost their way. Tilly's was the first most of Ashford ever saw.",
	"hallowed_hound": "Ellowen keeps the roads the dead walk home by, and the dogs that will not leave a grave are hers. A Shard fell in Ashford's churchyard on a hound that had lain three winters on its master's stone, and it did not make the dog alive again; it made it a Totem, which is a different thing. The Hallowed Hound is loyal to whoever it fights for now, completely, because it has already learned what loyalty costs and paid it.",
	# --- mystic, astral, umbral ------------------------------------------------------------
	"runeowl": "Aldrith's Shards fall on words. The Runeweaver's shrine at Solhaven keeps an owl, by tradition, to watch over the archive at night, and the one that was on duty when a Shard came through the window had been sleeping on the Book of Laws. The Runeowl has a page of it worked into each wing. It fights best in company, a word after a word; played alone it is only a bird that can read.",
	"starling": "Aster's Shards fall from very high and very seldom, and most of them are never found. One came down on a Sun Court astronomer's roof on the night she had finally mapped the new star, and found the starling nesting in her chimney. The Starling is a little of the sky with a fox's trickery in it: it has the Starwatcher's long view and a nest-thief's instinct for the right moment. It twinkles because it cannot help it.",
	"duskstalker": "Nocthra's magic does not fall; it rises, at the hour the lamps are lit. In Veilwild there was a cat that walked the walls at dusk and was never seen to come down, and the Veiled Mother's Shard found it between one lamp and the next. The Duskstalker is a shadow that has decided to hunt: swift, silent, and absent from any place you look for it. It answers to the Veiled Mother, and it keeps her habit of arriving when the light is going.",
}


## The Totem's story, or "" for a card that hasn't one (the Rites and Wards).
static func backstory(id: String) -> String:
	return String(BACKSTORY.get(id, ""))
